===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

The ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' interprets the character as {{liushu|p|adj=yes|nocap=yes}} of an animal. The bronze-script character clearly shows the {{zh-l|*虫}} component.

===Etymology===
;insect
:Compare {{cog|tbq-pro|*was||honey, bee}} ({{zh-ref|Benedict, 1972}}) or semantically closer {{w|Waic languages|Proto-Waic}} *''wak'' "insect" ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}), the latter from {{cog|mkh-pal-pro|*ʋaːk||insect, worm}} (Sidwell, 2015).
;Yu the Great
:The character is attested in bronze inscriptions from the late {{w|Shang dynasty}} ({{lang|zh|𫤵禹祖辛罍}}, {{lang|zh|𫤵禹祖辛卣}}).
:The earliest known reference to the legendary Yu in excavated texts is found in the {{lang|zh|豳公盨}} inscription from the mid-Western Zhou:
:{{zh-x|天 命 ^禹 敷 土，墮 山 濬{jùn} 川；乃 仇 方 設 正{zhèng}，降 民 監 德；|Heaven commanded '''Yu''' to spread out the land: he broke down the mountains and dredged the rivers, and thereupon established leaders for the various domains. [Heaven] sent down the people and kept watch over their virtue;|CL-PC|ref=Mid-Western Zhou (later phase), {{lang|zh|豳公盨}} inscription|collapsed=y}}
{{box-top}}
:In the {{lang|zh|禹盤}} (mid-Western Zhou), {{zh-l|*禹}} appears as the name of a contemporaneous individual:
:{{zh-x|^禹 拜 稽首，對 揚 王 休。^禹 作 文考 ^父-^癸 寶 䵼{pán} 彝，其 永 寶。|'''Yu''' bowed and prostrated, in response extolling the king's beneficence. '''Yu''' made this precious pan vessel for his late father Gui; may it be treasured forever.|CL-PC|ref=Mid-Western Zhou, {{lang|zh|禹盤}} inscription|collapsed=y}}
:In the late Western Zhou, the well-known {{lang|zh|禹鼎}} inscription ({{w|King Yi of Zhou|King Yi}} period) records the deeds of a military official named 禹 who led an expedition against the Marquis of È ({{lang|zh|鄂侯馭方}}):
:{{zh-x|^禹 曰：「丕 顯 桓桓 皇祖 ^穆-公 克 夾 詔 先王 奠 四方，肆 ^武-公 亦 弗 遐 忘 朕 聖 祖考 ^幽-大叔、^懿-叔，命 ^禹 纘{zuǎn} 朕 祖考，政{zhèng} 于 ^井 邦。肆 ^禹 亦 弗 敢 憃{chōng}，惕 共{gōng} 朕 辟 之 命。」|'''Yu''' said: "My greatly illustrious and martial august ancestor, Duke Mu, was able to assist the former kings in settling the four quarters. Thus Duke Wu also did not dare forget my sagely forefathers, the Great Uncle You and Uncle Yi, and commanded '''Yu''' to carry on [the work of] my ancestors, governing the state of Jing. Thus '''Yu''' did not dare act willfully, but reverently upheld the commands of my lord."|CL-PC|ref=Late Western Zhou ({{w|King Yi of Zhou|King Yi}} period), {{lang|zh|禹鼎}} inscription|collapsed=y}}
:The {{lang|zh|叔向父禹簋}} ({{w|King Li of Zhou|King Li}} period) records an individual of the same lineage (also referencing the ancestor {{lang|zh|幽大叔}}).
{{box-bottom}}

